//go:build !windows

package main

// FIXTURES FOR THE FOUR OFFICE ITEMS, on the same terms as
// contextoffice_defect_fixtures_test.go: each file is produced by a REAL writer,
// the property it exists for was VERIFIED in the package's own XML before any
// test was written against it, and each test re-asserts that property from the
// XML so the fixture cannot quietly stop exercising the bug.
//
// The lesson that makes this mandatory is recorded on fxXLSXSparseRow: a whole
// build's worth of green tests sat on top of three live defects because the
// fixtures happened not to contain the structures involved. Two of the four
// below have exactly that hazard, and it is measured rather than guessed:
// LibreOffice RENUMBERS slide parts when it writes a deck, so no
// LibreOffice-produced pptx can ever exhibit the reordering defect (see the
// measurement under fxPPTXReordered).
//
// ── fxPPTXReordered — a deck whose SLIDE ORDER IS NOT ITS FILE NUMBERING ─────
//
// Produced by python-pptx 1.0.2: three slides created in order, and
// then the LAST one moved to the FRONT by moving its <p:sldId> entry inside
// <p:sldIdLst>. That is precisely what PowerPoint does when a slide is dragged in
// the slide sorter — the list is rewritten, the slide PARTS are not touched and
// keep the numbers they were created with.
//
// VERIFIED PROPERTY, read out of the package:
//
//	ppt/presentation.xml   <p:sldIdLst><p:sldId r:id="rId9"/><p:sldId r:id="rId7"/>
//	                       <p:sldId r:id="rId8"/></p:sldIdLst>
//	presentation.xml.rels  rId7 -> slides/slide1.xml
//	                       rId8 -> slides/slide2.xml
//	                       rId9 -> slides/slide3.xml
//	slide1.xml  "…the deck's FIRST slide alpha"
//	slide2.xml  "…the deck's SECOND slide bravo"
//	slide3.xml  "…the deck's THIRD slide charlie"
//
// So the deck a person opens reads THIRD, FIRST, SECOND, and the file numbers say
// 1, 2, 3. MEASURED CONFIRMATION that this is the real order and not a python-pptx
// artifact: LibreOffice 26.2.5.2 was handed this exact file and asked to write it
// back out as pptx, and its slide1.xml is "…THIRD slide charlie", slide2.xml is
// "…FIRST slide alpha", slide3.xml is "…SECOND slide bravo". An office suite
// reading the file agrees with p:sldIdLst.
//
// That round trip is also WHY THIS FIXTURE COULD NOT BE MADE WITH LIBREOFFICE:
// LibreOffice renumbers the parts to match the order on write (its output's
// sldIdLst is rId3, rId4, rId5 in part order), so its files never diverge and
// would test nothing.
//
// ── fxDOCXComments — a Word document with REVIEWER COMMENTS ─────────────────
//
// Produced by python-docx 1.2.0: two body paragraphs, each with a
// comment anchored to it, by two different authors.
//
// VERIFIED PROPERTY, from the package:
//
//	word/_rels/document.xml.rels  rId9 type …/relationships/comments
//	                              -> comments.xml
//	word/comments.xml  <w:comment w:id="0" w:author="Dana Reviewer" w:initials="DR" …>
//	                     …<w:t>COMMENTBODY this number is stale, check it against
//	                     the finance sheet</w:t>…
//	                   <w:comment w:id="1" w:author="Sam Second" w:initials="SS" …>
//	                     …<w:t>COMMENTBODY second remark about the pricing change</w:t>…
//
// The comment bodies are NOT in word/document.xml — the body part carries only
// w:commentRangeStart / w:commentRangeEnd markers and an empty w:annotationRef
// run, which is why a reader of document.xml alone loses the words entirely
// rather than merely mis-placing them.
//
// ── fxXLSXHiddenSheet — a workbook with a HIDDEN and a VERY HIDDEN sheet ────
//
// Produced by openpyxl 3.1.5. Four sheets: two ordinary, one
// sheet_state="hidden" (what "Hide" in the sheet tab's menu does) and one
// sheet_state="veryHidden" (which Excel's own UI cannot unhide — it takes the VBA
// editor), so both states this format has are covered.
//
// VERIFIED PROPERTY, from xl/workbook.xml:
//
//	<sheet name="Summary"            state="visible"    r:id="rId1"/>
//	<sheet name="Scratch working"    state="hidden"     r:id="rId2"/>
//	<sheet name="Locked assumptions" state="veryHidden" r:id="rId3"/>
//	<sheet name="Notes"              state="visible"    r:id="rId4"/>
//
// A hidden sheet still has a full worksheet part with its cells in it; nothing
// about the part says it is hidden. The workbook's <sheet> entry is the only
// place that knows.
//
// ── fxPPTXHiddenSlide — a deck with a HIDDEN SLIDE ─────────────────────────
//
// Produced by LibreOffice 26.2.5.2 from a three-slide python-pptx
// deck whose middle slide was marked hidden the way PowerPoint's "Hide Slide"
// does it — show="0" on the p:sld root element. The fixture is LIBREOFFICE'S
// output, so the attribute in it is one an office suite wrote, not one that was
// only ever handed to it:
//
//	soffice -env:UserInstallation=file:///tmp/offfix/p1 --headless \
//	        --convert-to pptx --outdir hs hidslide_src.pptx
//
// VERIFIED PROPERTY, from the converted package:
//
//	ppt/slides/slide1.xml  <p:sld …>              "…first slide is visible alpha"
//	ppt/slides/slide2.xml  <p:sld … show="0" …>   "…second slide is HIDDEN bravo"
//	ppt/slides/slide3.xml  <p:sld …>              "…third slide is visible charlie"
//
// A hidden slide is skipped when the deck is presented and is still there when it
// is edited. Its text part is complete and ordinary.

